    "text": "To determine the total time Tyson spent in his races, we need to break down the problem into smaller steps.\n\n1. **Calculate the number of races in each location:**\n   Since half of his races happened in lakes and half in the ocean, Tyson had:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{10}{2} = 5 \\text{ races in the lake}\n   \\]\n   and\n   \\[\n   \\frac{10}{2} = 5 \\text{ races in the ocean}\n   \\]\n\n2. **Calculate the time spent in each type of race:**\n   - For the races in the lake, Tyson's speed is 3 miles per hour. The time spent in each lake race is:\n     \\[\n     \\frac{3 \\text{ miles}}{3 \\text{ miles per hour}} = 1 \\text{ hour}\n     \\]\n   - For the races in the ocean, Tyson's speed is 2.5 miles per hour. The time spent in each ocean race is:\n     \\[\n     \\frac{3 \\text{ miles}}{2.5 \\text{ miles per hour}} = \\frac{3}{2.5} = 1.2 \\text{ hours}\n     \\]\n\n3. **Calculate the total time spent in all races:**\n   - The total time spent in the lake races is:\n     \\[\n     5 \\text{ races} \\times 1 \\text{ hour per race} = 5 \\text{ hours}\n     \\]\n   - The total time spent in the ocean races is:\n     \\[\n     5 \\text{ races} \\times 1.2 \\text{ hours per race} = 6 \\text{ hours}\n     \\]\n   - Therefore, the total time spent in all races is:\n     \\[\n     5 \\text{ hours} + 6 \\text{ hours} = 11 \\text{ hours}\n     \\]\n\nThe final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{11}\n\\]",
